WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.426
Source evaluation asks two related questions: how trustworthy is the source,

00:00:04.426 --> 00:00:08.358
and how well does the specific information support the claim?

00:00:08.358 --> 00:00:11.504
A generally credible publisher can make an error,

00:00:11.504 --> 00:00:15.437
while an unfamiliar source may provide a verifiable primary document.

00:00:15.437 --> 00:00:16.616
Begin with origin.

00:00:16.616 --> 00:00:20.156
Identify who created the material, when it was produced,

00:00:20.156 --> 00:00:24.875
how it reached the current location, and whether the version is complete.

00:00:24.875 --> 00:00:28.414
Primary sources are closest to the event or record.

00:00:28.414 --> 00:00:30.380
Secondary sources interpret primary material.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:36.279
Recycled claims may look independent even when every article traces back to one unsupported statement.

00:00:36.279 --> 00:00:42.178
Corroboration is strongest when independent sources confirm different parts of a finding through separate evidence.

00:00:42.178 --> 00:00:44.144
Repetition alone is not corroboration.

00:00:44.144 --> 00:00:47.683
Analysts should trace claims backward, compare dates and details,

00:00:47.683 --> 00:00:51.222
and note whether sources could be copying one another.

00:00:51.222 --> 00:00:52.402
Timeliness affects meaning.

00:00:52.402 --> 00:00:55.548
Infrastructure, employment, ownership, locations, and online content change.

00:00:55.548 --> 00:00:59.481
Record the observation time and the time the source describes.

00:00:59.481 --> 00:01:05.379
An archived page may accurately show a past state but mislead if reported as current.

00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:08.525
Consider bias, incentives, access, expertise, and possible manipulation.

00:01:08.525 --> 00:01:14.031
Ask what the source could directly know, what it may want readers to believe,

00:01:14.031 --> 00:01:17.177
and whether evidence has been selected or altered.

00:01:17.177 --> 00:01:20.716
Evaluate internal consistency and look for credible contrary information.

00:01:20.716 --> 00:01:21.896
Avoid unsupported conclusions.

00:01:21.896 --> 00:01:25.042
Use calibrated language such as confirmed, likely, possible,

00:01:25.042 --> 00:01:27.401
or unverified according to organizational standards.

00:01:27.401 --> 00:01:30.547
Confidence should reflect source quality, corroboration, analytical consistency,

00:01:30.547 --> 00:01:33.300
and remaining gaps, not the analyst's enthusiasm.
